## 9. Other Important Terms

Your manual also mentions these terms.

### A. Null Error Rate

Null error rate measures how often a model would make incorrect predictions if it always predicted the majority class.

For example, suppose:
- 800 customers stayed.
- 200 customers left.

If the model always predicts "Stayed", it will be correct for 800 customers and incorrect for 200.

\[
Null\ Error\ Rate=\frac{200}{1000}=20\%
\]

### B. Accuracy Paradox

Accuracy paradox occurs when a model has high accuracy but performs poorly at identifying the minority class.

For example, if 95% of customers stay, a model that always predicts "Stay" achieves 95% accuracy but cannot identify customers who leave.

This is why we should also examine precision, recall and F1 Score.

